{"term":"erezione","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"fluids_states","subcategory":"erection","concept":"erection","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":8,"technical":75,"recognition_pct_current":99,"recognition_pct_peak":99,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Standard Italian word for erection; the engorgement and stiffening of erectile tissue, most commonly the penis, due to increased blood flow during sexual arousal.","english_term":"erection","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Used across all registers from clinical to casual; neither notably vulgar nor exclusively medical. The universal default term in Italian."}
{"term":"tumescenza","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"fluids_states","subcategory":"erection","concept":"penile_erection","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":1,"technical":96,"recognition_pct_current":25,"recognition_pct_peak":25,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Medical Italian term for tumescence; the state of penile engorgement and rigidity caused by vascular engorgement of erectile tissue, used almost exclusively in clinical and urological contexts.","english_term":"tumescence","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Rarely used outside medical or literary contexts; everyday Italian uses 'erezione' instead. May appear in formal health literature or pharmacological discussions of erectile dysfunction."}
{"term":"stare duro","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"fluids_states","subcategory":"erection","concept":"penile_erection","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":35,"technical":8,"recognition_pct_current":90,"recognition_pct_peak":90,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Colloquial Italian phrase meaning 'to be hard'; refers to having a penile erection, using the adjective 'duro' (hard/rigid) to describe the erect state.","english_term":"to be hard","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":""}
{"term":"avercelo duro","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"fluids_states","subcategory":"erection","concept":"penile_erection","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":48,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":85,"recognition_pct_peak":85,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Crude colloquial Italian phrase meaning 'to have it hard'; the clitic 'ce' (contracted ci+lo) refers implicitly to the penis, making the anatomical reference more explicit than the simpler 'stare duro'.","english_term":"to have it hard","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"The clitic pronoun 'ce' (unstressed form of 'ci' fused with 'lo') anchors the phrase to the penis as implicit referent, raising the crude register above the parallel phrase 'stare duro'."}
{"term":"incazzarsi","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"fluids_states","subcategory":"erection","concept":"erection","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":68,"technical":3,"recognition_pct_current":99,"recognition_pct_peak":99,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"In sexual contexts, to get an erection or become sexually aroused; etymologically derived from 'cazzo' (penis), meaning literally 'to get a cock'; in dominant modern Italian usage, however, the primary meaning is 'to get angry'.","english_term":"to get a boner","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Dual-meaning term: overwhelmingly used in modern Italian to mean 'to get angry'; the original physiological/sexual meaning (to become erect, to get turned on) is secondary in contemporary usage but present in explicit sexual speech. Recognition is near-universal, but predominantly for the anger sense."}
{"term":"stare in piedi","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"fluids_states","subcategory":"erection","concept":"penile_erection","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"euphemism","crude":15,"technical":3,"recognition_pct_current":78,"recognition_pct_peak":78,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Euphemistic Italian phrase literally meaning 'to stand upright' or 'to be on one's feet'; used as a sexual euphemism for having a penile erection, exploiting the dual meaning of vertical standing and penile uprightness.","english_term":"to stand up","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Works as a euphemism by mapping the spatial concept of verticality onto penile erection; 'ce la fai a stare in piedi?' ('can you manage to stay hard?') is a representative sexual use. Mild enough to appear in innuendo contexts."}
{"term":"essere bagnata","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"fluids_states","subcategory":"vaginal_fluid","concept":"vaginal_lubrication","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":30,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":92,"recognition_pct_peak":92,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Colloquial Italian phrase meaning 'to be wet'; refers to the state of vaginal lubrication during female sexual arousal, by direct analogy with the English slang 'to be wet'.","english_term":"to be wet","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"The grammatically feminine adjective 'bagnata' specifies a female subject; the male equivalent ('essere bagnato') exists but rarely carries this sexual connotation."}
{"term":"latte","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"fluids_states","subcategory":"semen","concept":"semen","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"euphemism","crude":18,"technical":2,"recognition_pct_current":70,"recognition_pct_peak":70,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The standard Italian word for milk used as a sexual euphemism for semen; the comparison is based on the white, viscous appearance of the fluid.","english_term":"milk","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Entirely neutral outside sexual contexts; the sexual euphemism is widely understood especially in explicit material, dirty jokes, and casual crude speech. Also used in the phrase 'fare il latte' (to ejaculate)."}
{"term":"spruzzo","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"fluids_states","subcategory":"ejaculation","concept":"ejaculation","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":42,"technical":8,"recognition_pct_current":82,"recognition_pct_peak":82,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Italian slang for a spurt or squirt of ejaculate; refers both to the jet of semen expelled at ejaculation and, by extension, to the act of ejaculation itself.","english_term":"squirt","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Primary meaning is any spray or splatter of liquid (paint, water, etc.); the sexual sense is well established in crude speech. Can also be applied to female ejaculation, though this is secondary."}
{"term":"sbobba","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"fluids_states","subcategory":"semen","concept":"semen","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":70,"technical":2,"recognition_pct_current":52,"recognition_pct_peak":52,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Crude Italian slang for semen; literally 'slop' or 'watery gruel', applied to semen by invoking the same disgust register as the primary meaning based on perceived consistency and unpalatability.","english_term":"jizz","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Less common than 'sborra' in contemporary usage. The primary meaning remains watery, tasteless food; the sexual transfer is based on texture/appearance analogy and deliberate degradation."}
{"term":"eccitato","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"arousal","concept":"sexual_arousal","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":15,"technical":28,"recognition_pct_current":99,"recognition_pct_peak":99,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Italian adjective meaning 'excited' or 'aroused' (masculine form); in sexual contexts describes a state of sexual arousal or heightened desire in a male subject.","english_term":"aroused","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Also the standard word for excited in non-sexual contexts (excited about an event, gift, etc.); sexual meaning is entirely context-dependent and does not feel crude when used clinically or between partners."}
{"term":"eccitata","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"arousal","concept":"sexual_arousal","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":15,"technical":28,"recognition_pct_current":99,"recognition_pct_peak":99,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Italian adjective meaning 'excited' or 'aroused' (feminine form); in sexual contexts describes a state of sexual arousal or heightened desire in a female subject.","english_term":"aroused","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Feminine grammatical form of 'eccitato'; identical range of sexual and non-sexual uses."}
{"term":"arrapato","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"arousal","concept":"sexual_arousal","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":60,"technical":3,"recognition_pct_current":95,"recognition_pct_peak":95,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Crude Italian slang adjective meaning 'horny' or 'sexually turned on' (masculine form); describes a state of intense and conspicuous sexual arousal.","english_term":"horny","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Etymology debated: possibly from dialectal 'rapa' (turnip) as phallic metaphor, or from a Ligurian/southern root. Also used as a reflexive verb 'arraparsi' (to get horny). Among the most common Italian crude arousal terms."}
{"term":"arrapata","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"arousal","concept":"sexual_arousal","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":60,"technical":3,"recognition_pct_current":95,"recognition_pct_peak":95,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Crude Italian slang adjective meaning 'horny' or 'sexually turned on' (feminine form); describes a state of intense sexual arousal in a female subject.","english_term":"horny","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Feminine grammatical form of 'arrapato'; equally crude and equally common. Applying this term to a woman carries no additional stigma beyond the inherent crudeness."}
{"term":"in calore","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"arousal","concept":"animal_lust","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":48,"technical":8,"recognition_pct_current":90,"recognition_pct_peak":90,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Italian phrase literally meaning 'in heat'; applied to humans to describe intense, animal-like sexual arousal, borrowing the veterinary term for mammalian estrus and implying loss of rational control over sexual urges.","english_term":"in heat","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Technically a biology and veterinary term for estrus; when applied to humans it carries a deliberately dehumanizing, animalistic connotation. The implication of uncontrolled, instinct-driven desire is central to the sexual meaning."}
{"term":"voglia","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"desire","concept":"lust","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":12,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":99,"recognition_pct_peak":99,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Italian noun meaning 'desire', 'craving', or 'urge'; in sexual contexts refers to sexual desire or the state of being in the mood for sex, as in 'avere voglia' (to feel the sexual urge).","english_term":"desire","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Entirely neutral outside sexual contexts ('voglia di dormire' = urge to sleep); in sexual contexts it is mild and nearly euphemistic. The phrase 'avere voglia di qualcuno' explicitly means to sexually desire someone."}
{"term":"caldo","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"arousal","concept":"sexual_arousal","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":22,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":78,"recognition_pct_peak":78,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Italian adjective meaning 'hot' or 'warm' (masculine form); in sexual slang, describes a person who is sexually aroused or has high sexual appetite.","english_term":"hot","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Primary meaning is the thermal adjective; the sexual sense ('essere caldo' = to be horny/to be sexually willing) is a secondary but well-understood meaning in context. Less crude than 'arrapato'."}
{"term":"calda","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"arousal","concept":"sexual_arousal","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":22,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":78,"recognition_pct_peak":78,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Italian adjective meaning 'hot' or 'warm' (feminine form); in sexual slang, describes a woman who is sexually aroused, receptive, or of notably high sexual appetite.","english_term":"hot","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Feminine form of 'caldo'; when applied to a woman ('è calda', 'una donna calda') it can specifically imply she is in a state of arousal or sexually forward. Slightly more likely to carry objectifying connotation when applied to women than the masculine form to men."}
{"term":"lussurioso","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"desire","concept":"lasciviousness","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":30,"technical":25,"recognition_pct_current":62,"recognition_pct_peak":62,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Italian literary adjective meaning 'lustful'; describes a person dominated by sexual desire, derived from 'lussuria', the deadly sin of Lust in Catholic moral theology.","english_term":"lustful","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Carries a heavy literary and Catholic moral-theological overtone: 'lussuria' is one of the seven deadly sins, giving the term weight in Italian culture beyond mere slang. Rarely used in modern informal speech; preferred in literature, preaching, and formal moral discourse."}
{"term":"lascivo","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"desire","concept":"lasciviousness","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":33,"technical":28,"recognition_pct_current":58,"recognition_pct_peak":58,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Italian literary adjective meaning 'lascivious'; describes a person or demeanor characterized by sexually immoral or licentious tendencies, derived directly from Latin 'lascivus'.","english_term":"lascivious","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Borrowed from Latin 'lascivus' (playful, wanton); found primarily in literary, classical, or elevated moral discourse. In modern everyday Italian, 'arrapato' or 'eccitato' are preferred; 'lascivo' survives mainly in formal writing, art criticism, and ironic usage."}
